    "last_assistant_text": "No crash. Let me try a fundamentally different approach. Let me try to create inputs where genjmp2 with EXT1 and JMPLINK_START is called, and then look at whether dispatch_linked causes writes to emit_B that extend the buffer to exactly the right size for an OOB.\n\nActually, wait. Let me re-examine the whole chain more carefully with specific numbers. From the debug output:\n- genjmp2 EXT1: a=257, pc=JMPLINK_START, pos=524, s->pc=526, icapa=1024\n\nAfter this, more codegen happens (the right side of `&&`), pushing pc further. Let's say by the time dispatch is called, pc=530 and icapa=1024.\n\ndispatch(s, 524):\n- pos1 = 526\n- offset = 530 - 526 = 4\n- reads PEEK_S(iseq+524) → 0xFFFF → newpos = -1\n- writes offset=4 at 524 (bytes: 0x00, 0x04)\n- newpos ≠ 0, returns 526 + (-1) = 525\n\ndispatch_linked calls dispatch(s, 525):\n- pos1 = 527  \n- offset = 530 - 527 = 3\n- reads PEEK_S(iseq+525) → (iseq[525]<<8 | iseq[526])\n\niseq[525] was the low byte of the offset just written (0x04), and iseq[526] is whatever was there before (first byte of the next instruction after genjmp2).\n\nPEEK_S = (0x04 << 8) | iseq[526]. So newpos = (int16_t)(0x0400 | iseq[526]).\n\nwrites offset=3 at 525 (bytes: 0x00, 0x03)\nreturns 527 + newpos. newpos is probably large (> 1000) which would put us somewhere > 1527 in the buffer. Since icapa=1024, that's WAY past the buffer → ASAN would NOT normally catch this because we'd be accessing virtual memory that's part of the heap.\n\nWait, actually ASAN uses redzones around heap allocations. The iseq buffer is allocated with mrb_realloc_simple which calls realloc. ASAN instruments this and places redzones after the allocation. Reading past icapa*sizeof(mrb_code) would trigger ASAN.\n\nBut the position 527 + newpos could be very large (e.g., 1551). `s->iseq + 1551` would be past the 1024-byte allocation. ASAN should catch this!\n\nLet me verify this with my test binary.",
